  "raw_answer": "C1| Dilated cardiomyopathy can be caused by idiopathic factors, familial TTN gene mutations, myocarditis, thiamine deficiency, Chagas disease, alcohol abuse, certain drugs (such as doxorubicin and cocaine), and peripartum pregnancy. |Passage 1|\n\nC2| Myocarditis, which may arise from viral (e.g., adenovirus, coxsackie B, parvovirus B19, HIV, HHV-6), parasitic (e.g., Trypanosoma cruzi, Toxoplasma gondii), bacterial (e.g., Borrelia burgdorferi, Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Corynebacterium diphtheriae), toxin (e.g., carbon monoxide, black widow venom), rheumatic fever, drug (e.g., doxorubicin, cocaine), or autoimmune (e.g., Kawasaki disease, sarcoidosis, SLE, polymyositis/dermatomyositis) origins, is a cause of dilated cardiomyopathy. |Passage 3|",
  "overall": {
